course: dictionary
lesson: 13632
title: A word for persuading
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An inducement is something that persuades you to do something. It might be a prize, a treat or some money. «The shop gave every child a free sticker as an inducement to join the club.» The sticker is the reward, and the reward is why the children joined.
explain: Look at the words around inducement to work out its meaning. Words like «offered», «reward» and «to join» are clues. Here, the sticker is given to get the children to do something, so an inducement must be something that encourages people.
explain: Inducements can be fair or wrong. A bonus for hard work is fair. A secret payment to make someone break the rules is a bribe, and that is a wrong kind of inducement. «The guard refused the money, because it was an unfair inducement.» Always ask: was the reward offered honestly?
explain: To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence. Try «something that encourages» in place of inducement. If the sentence still makes sense, you have found the right meaning.
fact: An inducement is something that persuades or encourages someone to do something.
fact: An inducement can be a reward, such as a prize or a bonus.
fact: An inducement can also be a bribe, which is a wrong kind of reward given to get someone to break the rules.
fact: «An inducement to» is often followed by the thing you want someone to do, such as «an inducement to stay».
fact: «Financial inducement» means an inducement involving money.
exercise: «The shop gave every child a free sticker as an inducement to join the club.» What does inducement mean here? | A map of the shop | A club rule that must be obeyed | *Something that encourages you to do something | A kind of sticker
exercise: «Mum offered me extra pocket money as an inducement to tidy my room.» What was the inducement? | *Extra pocket money | Tidying my room | Mum | A cup of tea
exercise: Which of these is a wrong kind of inducement? | A bonus for working hard | A prize for winning a race | A free sticker for joining | *Money paid secretly to make someone break the rules
test: «The company offered a large bonus as an inducement to stay.» What does inducement mean? | A bill to pay | A sort of tool | *Something that encourages someone to do something | Something that makes someone leave
test: Which phrase means an offer of money to persuade someone? | An inducement to | A quiet inducement | A bright inducement | *A financial inducement
exam: Which sentence uses inducement correctly? | The inducement went to the park for a picnic. | I inducement my dog to sleep. | *The team offered a prize as an inducement to play harder. | The inducement was a bright blue shirt.
